[00:29.03]Conversation One
[00:30.65]M: As many college students are thinking about making use of their long summer to do some meaningful stuff and participating in some volunteering projects, we invite Omie Dale, the spokeswoman of AIESEC, to our program. 
[00:45.26]Now, Omie, how would you describe AIESEC?
[00:49.23]W: AIESEC is a global, non-governmental youth organization, and it is present in 126 countries around the world and focuses on youth empowerment. 
[00:59.67]It provides a platform for young people to develop their leadership and professional skills, with the backdrop of aiming towards creating positive social impact.
[01:08.02]M: Wonderful! What kind of projects can students apply for?
[01:11.53]W: All the projects come under three general umbrellas: Global Volunteer, Global Entrepreneur and Global Talent. 
[01:17.73]The most popular is Global Volunteer, which as the name suggests is an unpaid volunteering experience, which typically lasts 6 to 8 weeks. 
[01:25.83]Global Entrepreneur offers placements with start-up companies and typically lasts 6 to 12 weeks. 
[01:31.39]And Global Talent is longer, paid internships with companies¡ªoften big names such as Nike.
[01:37.23]M: So, the program at least lasts for 6 weeks?
[01:40.08]W: Technically it's 6 weeks, but there are exceptions where it can be 4, 
[01:43.54]for example during winter break when there isn't this amount of time to spare.
[01:47.50]M: Reasonable. Any advice on how to choose a project?
[01:50.82]W: Make sure you really know what you want to do, and where you want to go. 
[01:54.33]There are so many opportunities and it's easy to pick the first one you find, 
[01:58.75]but you're going to be away for a while, so it's important to choose something that aligns with your interest.
[02:04.10]M: That's true. 
[02:05.13]Thank you, Omie, for all the information.
[02:06.82]W: You're welcome.
[02:07.90]Questions 1 to 4 are based on the conversation you have just heard.
[02:12.13]1. What is the goal of AIESEC?
[02:30.28]2. Which project can provide students with internships in big companies?
[02:47.47]3. How long does an AIESEC program last?
[03:06.51]4. How should a student make his choice among AIESEC projects?
[03:26.39]Conversation Two
[03:27.78]W: Good morning, everyone. 
[03:28.98]First, let me introduce our guest, Dr. Tommy Lundberg, an exercise researcher and author of The Physiology of Resistance Training. 
[03:36.97]Dr. Lundberg, welcome to our show.
[03:39.07]M: My pleasure.
[03:40.09]W: Dr. Lundberg, why is resistance training important to us?
[03:43.41]M: Resistance training is also known as strength training. 
[03:46.50]Whether performed with weights, machines or your own body weight, it is important for your long-term health. 
[03:53.34]It increases muscular strength, endurance and bone density.
[03:57.50]W: Hmm. Sounds really necessary. 
[03:59.68]But as far as I know, not many people do resistance training regularly.
[04:03.79]M: Yes. A mere 30.2% of Americans met these guidelines, according to a 2017 data analysis. 
[04:11.56]In the same study, nearly 60% of participants said they did no strength training at all.
[04:16.75]W: Dr. Lundberg, do you yourself do resistance training often?
[04:19.96]M: I don't have time for long sessions, so I do frequent, very short sessions¡ªoften 10 or 15 minutes of training. 
[04:26.73]You can get good health effects, and even good training results, with short sessions.
[04:31.47]W: What's your suggestion for the average person?
[04:33.86]M: The general recommendation is to do muscle-strengthening activities two times per week, including all of the large muscle groups. 
[04:40.92]If you want to be time-effective, do one or two sets of each exercise. 
[04:45.47]If you have more time, do three or four. 
[04:47.80]But it's always better to reduce the number of sets per exercise than to reduce the number of exercises.
[04:53.62]W: What else is important to know on this topic?
[04:56.09]M: It's not enough to advocate for exercise. 
[04:58.52]We need to build physical activity and exercise into the environment. 
[05:03.29]We need to have more outdoor gyms, maybe more physical activity in school. 
[05:08.20]It has to be easier to take the stairs instead of the escalator. 
[05:12.06]That perspective is important if you really want to change public health.
[05:15.84]W: It seems that we all should take some strength training. 
[05:18.71]Thank you again, Dr. Lundberg, for coming here.
[05:20.99]M: Glad talking to you.
[05:23.67]Questions 5 to 8 are based on the conversation you have just heard.
[05:27.87]5. What does the man say about resistance training?
[05:44.62]6. How many Americans do resistance training on a regular basis?
[06:03.14]7. How does the man do resistance training himself?
[06:20.00]8. What does the man suggest common people do?
