[07:02.15]Passage One
[07:03.55]Sea ice in the Antarctic area fell to a record low this year. 
[07:07.52]The drop is a result of rising temperatures worldwide. 
[07:10.95]And there is no quick fix to make up for the damage, scientists said recently in a new study.
[07:16.12]Antarctica's minimum summer ice cover, which last year dropped below 2 million square kilometers for the first time since satellite monitoring began in 1978, fell further to a new low in February.
[07:28.94]Anna Hogg of the University of Leeds in Britain was one of the study's co-writers. 
[07:33.79]When speaking about the melting icebergs and shelves, she said ¡°It's going to take decades if not centuries for these things to recover.¡±
[07:41.52]This year's sea ice minimum is 20 percent lower than the average over the last 40 years. 
[07:47.20]The sea ice loss represents an area nearly 10 times that of New Zealand, said Tim Naish of the Antarctic Research Center at New Zealand's Victoria University of Wellington. 
[07:57.38]Naish added that in some cases we are getting close to important large changes that could lead to serious consequences for future generations.
[08:06.36]The warming of Earth's surface driven by the burning of fossil fuels has made Antarctica more likely to suffer from extreme events. 
[08:14.09]And the effect is almost ¡°certain¡± to get worse. 
[08:16.88]Climate change will lead to increases in the size and frequency of heatwaves, ice shelf collapses and declines in sea ice.
[08:24.97]Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.
[08:28.85]9. When did the satellite monitoring of Antarctica's ice cover begin?
[08:47.20]10. What does Anna Hogg say about the loss of ice cover in Antarctica?
[09:05.89]11. What is highly likely to happen to Antarctica?
[09:21.96]Passage Two
[09:24.12]Some of the deadliest diseases to infect humans have come from animals. 
[09:28.06]The virus that causes AIDS, for example, came from chimpanzees. 
[09:31.73]But, as a new study shows, this disease exchange has not been just from animals to humans. 
[09:36.69]In fact, the research produced a surprising result: humans give about two times as many viruses to animals than they give to us. 
[09:44.32]The researchers looked at nearly 12 million sets of viruses and found almost 3,000 times a virus jumped from one species to another. 
[09:52.01]Of the 21% that involved humans, 64% were human-to-animal transmissions and 36% were animal-to-human transmissions. 
[10:00.81]The study represents humans' large ¡°impact on the environment and the animals around us,¡± said Cedric Tan, a lead writer of the study.
[10:08.51]Viruses go between species in the same ways as viruses are transmitted between humans. 
[10:13.57]That includes ¡°direct contact with infected fluids, or getting bitten by other species, amongst others.¡± 
[10:19.23]But before the virus enters a different species, it must either have ¡°the biological toolkit, or acquire host-specific adaptations, to enter the cells of the new host species and exploit their resources,¡± Tan added.
[10:32.25]Over thousands of years, pandemics that have killed millions of people have been caused by viruses, bacteria and fungi that crossed over to people from animals. 
[10:40.86]However, researchers also say that in most cases, such infections lead nowhere, as the virus is poorly adapted and there is no onward transmission in the new host.
[10:50.82]Questions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.
[10:55.14]12. For what purpose does the speaker mention AIDS?
[11:12.46]13. What is the finding of the new study?
[11:28.61]14. What kinds of virus can infect a new host species?
[11:47.53]15. In most cases, what happens when a virus goes into a new species?
