[12:32.14]Recording One
[12:33.58]A happier life after getting married may not just be in fairy tales. 
[12:37.57]It's in the data, too. 
[12:39.06]Adults who are married report being far happier than those in any other relationship status, according to a Gallup Poll published Friday.
[12:46.27]From 2009 to 2023, more than 2.5 million adults in the United States were asked how they would rate their current life, with zero being the worst possible rating and 10 being the highest. 
[12:58.00]Then the researchers asked respondents what they anticipated their happiness level would be in five years. 
[13:04.12]To be considered thriving, a person had to rank their current life as a seven or higher and their anticipated future as an eight or higher, according to the survey. 
[13:12.84]Over the survey period, married people consistently reported their happiness levels higher than their unmarried counterparts, ranging from 12% to 24% higher depending on the year, according to the data.
[13:24.89]The gap was there even when researchers adjusted for factors such as age, race, ethnicity, gender and education. 
[13:31.36]Education is a strong predictor of happiness, but the data showed that married adults who did not attend high school evaluate their lives more favorably than unmarried adults with a graduate degree.
[13:42.03]According to Ian Kerner, a licensed marriage and family therapist, maybe the happiness tied to marriage has something to do with what people expect from it. 
[13:49.91]In his practice over the last decade he's noticed a gradual shift from the ¡°romantic marriage¡± to the ¡°companionate marriage,¡± meaning that people are increasingly choosing spouses at the outset who are like best friends.
[14:01.37]There is a lot that we can learn from the data, but marriage is not the only reason for higher levels of happiness. 
[14:07.14]It could be that people who have qualities that tend to lead to more consistent happiness are also those who would seek out marriage. 
[14:14.10]The quality of marriages, however, can vary based on individual circumstances, societal changes and the cultural outlook on marriage. 
[14:21.37]For example, in communities where marriage is often a practical necessity, the data shows a smaller effect on happiness than in those where individuals feel more able to choose their status and partner.
[14:32.41]Whether married or dating, you can optimize your chances for a happy relationship by communicating well on what your commitment to one another entails.
[14:41.55]Questions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.
[14:45.94]16. What do we learn about the Gallup Poll survey about marriage?
[15:04.23]17. What does Ian Kerner say about marriages of today?
[15:21.79]18. What does the speaker say about the quality of marriages?
[15:40.79]Recording Two
[15:42.65]When it comes to a mom's mental health, grandparents are a big deal, according to a new study. 
[15:49.02]It is said that mothers are less likely to purchase pills for depression if their own parents are younger than 70, employed and do not have severe health problems.
[15:58.77]Researchers tracked 488,000 mothers of young children in Finland between 2000 and 2014. 
[16:06.37]The research team collected data including whether the mother had a partner or was separated as well as the age, health, distance and employment status of the grandparents.
[16:16.58]The connection between access to grandparents' help and lower use of anti-depression drug was especially strong in mothers who were separated from their partners. 
[16:25.67]As the researcher said, grandparents are an important source of support to families with children, and support from others is well-known to protect individuals from depression. 
[16:35.70]As the association between maternal depression and adverse child outcomes is well established, support not only matters for the mothers' well-being but also to the growing numbers of children living with separated mothers.
[16:48.21]There were instances when grandparents might have a negative impact on the mother's health. 
[16:52.96]The highest anti-depression drug use was in moms who had parents or in-laws who were older, in poor health or living far away. 
[17:00.97]The researchers hypothesized that the grandparents were not able to provide support to the parent in those situations, and instead the parent likely faced increased stress to take care of the older adults.
[17:12.24]Researchers noted that the research was done in Finland, which has generous access to health care and childcare, so the support families got from older generations may extend beyond those needs. 
[17:23.89]These other needs can look like having someone to talk to, having reinforcements when you need to run out of the house with no one to take care of your child. 
[17:32.04]While such support may come from grandparents, families who don't have that help aren't at a loss.
[17:37.19]So, what should people do if they really can't get help from their family members? 
[17:41.67]Going out into the community to seek help from social services, researchers said. 
[17:46.47]Even the Internet can help. 
[17:48.50]Talking in groups on social media with other parents and caregivers may not give you the support you need, but it can help connect you with friends or parenting groups in your area. 
[17:59.29]Putting yourself out there early may save you from feeling isolated and alone later on.
[18:05.14]Questions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.
[18:10.37]19. What does the speaker mainly talk about?
[18:26.78]20. What kinds of grandparents have a bad impact on the mothers?
[18:44.39]21. What should mothers do if the grandparents are of little help?
[19:01.99]Recording Three
[19:03.55]Several years ago, after some major losses and personal setbacks, I gave up entirely on making New Year's resolutions. 
[19:11.66]I had spent most of my 20s setting ambitious goals each January. 
[19:15.88]By the time each December rolled around, I would take stock of my plans for the past 12 months and discover that most had fallen short. 
[19:24.21]So, I stopped making resolutions. 
[19:26.54]Considering that most New Year's resolutions fail by mid-March, this felt like a clever thing to do. 
[19:32.62]If my goals were likely to fail, and if life could be so cruel, why even bother?
[19:37.78]To my surprise, I soon missed the benefits of setting goals. 
[19:41.93]The start of a new year is a prime opportunity for self-improvement¡ªfor letting go of the past and embracing new, healthier habits. 
[19:49.98]That's why, for the past few years, I've approached resolutions differently. 
[19:54.42]I've found a way to set goals that are realistic, compassionate and flexible. 
[19:59.15]So, here's my solution: Instead of setting a series of resolutions for the year, or even a monthlong goal, try taking things week by week.
[20:08.49]Since January 2022, I, together with a friend, have kept a shared document in which we set an intention for the week ahead and reflect on the previous seven days. 
[20:20.32]Instead of naming big, ambitious, long-term goals, we opt for smaller, more manageable intentions each week. 
[20:27.54]Each Sunday, we set a new goal for the week ahead and reflect on how the previous week went. 
[20:33.24]We also have a prompt to reflect on one good moment from the past week. 
[20:38.10]More often than not, the intentions we make each week are small. 
[20:42.02]In 2023, I vowed to do things such as go for daily walks, go to bed early, and stop drinking alcohol. 
[20:50.83]If I made a promise to do these things every day, all year long, I'd undoubtedly fall short. 
[20:56.74]But aiming for small habits one week at a time keeps those goals achievable.
[21:01.68]Setting weekly intentions has also helped me complete dull but important tasks I may otherwise put off. 
[21:08.42]I'll be the first to admit that these aren't exciting tasks, but I can confidently say that setting weekly intentions has helped me create a foundation for a healthy, grounded, gratitude-filled life.
[21:19.75]By now, your New Year's resolutions may have already fallen short, you may have experienced an unforeseen setback, or your motivation may have started to flag. 
[21:29.48]That's OK! The good news is that next week is just around the corner. 
[21:33.63]There's a new opportunity to begin again.
[21:36.87]Questions 22 to 25 are based on the recording you have just heard.
[21:41.54]22. Why did the speaker give up making New Year's resolutions?
[21:58.47]23. What surprised the speaker after he stopped setting goals?
[22:16.32]24. What does the speaker say about his weekly goals?
[22:34.22]25. What benefit does the speaker get from setting weekly goals?
[22:51.57]That¡¯s the end of listening comprehension.